ery good leadership, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, can be grumpy, can be uncooperative. Wait note: In my notes, B can be grumpy and uncooperative (different from Emily's note about pretentious). So we have additional concerns: B can be grumpy, uncooperative. That is crew cooperation issue. So we could challenge B's suitability due to cooperation concerns, and suggest another candidate maybe C with positive crew atmosphere. But C has egocentric and communication difficulty. D has arrogance and weak leadership. A has leadership but unorganized and not good at criticism. We need to bring up a point. Perhaps we can say we have concerns about B's crew cooperation (grumpy, uncooperative) and suggest we need to compare with others. So we might not change vote yet but ask for more info on others. According to process, we should make a focused argumentative contribution: maybe we say we see B's strengths but also note his uncooperative behavior, which could be a serious issue for crew cooperation, and we need to weigh that against other candidates. So we could ask a question to James Carter about Candidate A's reliability or stress handling. Or ask about Candidate C's decision quality. But we can also just state our current provisional recommendation maybe still B but with caveat.\n\nWe need to include metadata vote. Should we vote B? We could still vote B but note concerns. That aligns with \"current recommendation\". So we can say I still recommend B but need more info on others. Provide evidence: B is very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), but can be grumpy and uncooperative (crew cooperation). So we need to weigh that. So we can say I remain with B but we should explore others.\n\nThus produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: mention Emily's point, add my concerns about B's cooperation, and note that other candidates